          <td class="py-4 px-3 sm:px-4 text-right font-black text-gray-950">$21.00</td>
          <td class="py-4 px-3 sm:px-4 text-right font-black text-gray-950">$31.50</td>
          <td class="py-4 px-4 sm:px-6 text-right font-bold text-gray-800">11.67¢ / mi</td>
        </tr>

        <!-- Benchmark 30 MPG Gas Car @ $4.40 -->
        <tr class="bg-gray-50/80">
          <td class="py-4 px-4 sm:px-6">
            <strong class="text-gray-950 block">⛽ 30 MPG Gas Car (@ $4.40 / Gallon)</strong>
            <span class="text-[11px] text-gray-500">High-cost West Coast / urban gas station price</span>
          </td>
          <td class="py-4 px-3 sm:px-4 text-center font-semibold text-gray-700">$4.40 / gal</td>
          <td class="py-4 px-3 sm:px-4 text-right font-black text-red-700">$26.40</td>
          <td class="py-4 px-3 sm:px-4 text-right font-black text-red-700">$39.60</td>
          <td class="py-4 px-4 sm:px-6 text-right font-bold text-red-700">14.67¢ / mi</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    <strong>The Critical Takeaway:</strong> Notice the staggering price disparity between non-member fast charging and gasoline. At <strong>$0.58/kWh</strong>, replenishing 50 kWh costs <strong>$29.00 (16.57¢/mile)</strong>—making it <strong>$8.00 more expensive</strong> than putting 6 gallons of gas into a 30 MPG car ($21.00)!
  </p>

  <p class="mb-8 leading-relaxed">
    However, when charging at home on an off-peak rate ($0.08/kWh), that exact same 180 miles of driving costs just <strong>$4.40</strong>—delivering an extraordinary <strong>$16.60 in savings per 180 miles</strong> compared to the gas car.
  </p>

  <!-- Section 3: Break-Even Math for the $12.99 Membership -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    3. The $12.99/Month Non-Tesla Membership: Exact Break-Even Math
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    For drivers of non-Tesla electric vehicles (Ford F-150 Lightning, Mustang Mach-E, Chevy Equinox EV, Rivian R1T/R1S, Hyundai Ioniq 5), Tesla offers an optional subscription inside the Tesla mobile app for <strong>$12.99 per month</strong> (or an annual option in select regions).
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    This membership grants non-Tesla drivers access to the exact same discounted rates paid by Tesla owners, saving an average of <strong>$0.16 to $0.18 per kWh</strong> on every charging session.
  </p>

  <!-- Math Callout Box -->
  <div class="my-6 p-6 rounded-3xl bg-slate-900 text-white not-prose shadow-lg border border-slate-800">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-3">
      <span class="w-7 h-7 rounded-lg bg-emerald-500 text-slate-950 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-black">
        <i class="fa-solid fa-calculator"></i>
      </span>
      <h3 class="text-sm font-black uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400">The Exact Mathematical Formula</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm space-y-2 text-slate-200 font-medium">
      <p>Average Non-Member Rate: <strong>$0.58 / kWh</strong></p>
      <p>Discounted Member Rate: <strong>$0.41 / kWh</strong></p>
      <p>Net Savings Per Kilowatt-Hour: <strong>$0.17 / kWh</strong></p>
      <div class="p-3 my-2 bg-slate-800 rounded-xl font-mono text-emerald-300 text-sm font-bold">
        Break-Even Point = $12.99 ÷ $0.17/kWh = 76.41 kWh
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-300 text-xs">
        <strong>Conclusion:</strong> You must purchase at least <strong>76.4 kWh per month</strong> from Tesla Superchargers for the membership to pay for itself. Any additional kilowatt-hour charged beyond 76.4 kWh is pure profit in your wallet.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-950 mt-6 mb-3">What Does 76.4 kWh Mean in the Real World?</h3>
  <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-6 text-gray-800 font-medium">
    <li><strong>Ford F-150 Lightning (131 kWh Extended Pack):</strong> A single 15% to 80% road-trip charging stop consumes <strong>85.1 kWh</strong>. You break even and save money on your <em>very first charging stop</em>!</li>
    <li><strong>Chevy Equinox EV (85 kWh Pack):</strong> A typical 10% to 80% fast-charge top-up draws <strong>59.5 kWh</strong>. You break even after just <strong>1.28 charging sessions</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Rivian R1T / R1S (135 kWh Large Pack):</strong> A single 20% to 80% highway session draws <strong>81 kWh</strong>. Breaks even in a single stop.</li>
    <li><strong>Hyundai Ioniq 5 / Kia EV6 (84 kWh Pack):</strong> Draws roughly <strong>58.8 kWh</strong> during a highway stop, reaching break-even in 1.3 sessions.</li>
  </ul>

