  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    6. Frequently Asked Questions: Tesla Supercharging Costs &amp; Memberships
  </h2>
  
  <div class="space-y-4 my-8 not-prose">
    <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-base font-black text-gray-950 mb-2">How much does a Tesla Supercharger cost per kWh in late 2026?</h3>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        In late 2026, Tesla Supercharger rates in the United States average $0.40 to $0.43 per kWh for Tesla owners and subscribed non-Tesla members. For non-Tesla drivers charging pay-as-you-go without a membership, rates average $0.57 to $0.60 per kWh, with high-demand urban and peak-hour stations in California, New York, and Massachusetts reaching $0.63 to $0.72+ per kWh.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-base font-black text-gray-950 mb-2">Is the $12.99 per month Tesla Supercharging membership worth it for non-Tesla EVs?</h3>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Yes, if you charge at Superchargers at least twice a month or take a single multi-state road trip. The non-Tesla membership costs $12.99/month and discounts your rate by roughly $0.17 per kWh (from ~$0.58/kWh down to ~$0.41/kWh). The exact mathematical break-even point is 76.4 kWh per month ($12.99 ÷ $0.17). A single 10% to 80% charging session on an EV with an 80–90 kWh battery draws 56 to 65 kWh, meaning you break even in just 1.2 to 1.5 charging sessions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-base font-black text-gray-950 mb-2">Is Tesla Supercharging more expensive than gasoline?</h3>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        At non-member rates ($0.57–$0.60/kWh), Supercharging is actually more expensive per mile than fueling a fuel-efficient 30–35 MPG gas car at $3.50/gal. At 3.5 miles/kWh, a non-member paying $0.58/kWh spends approximately 16.6¢ per mile, while a 30 MPG gas car at $3.50/gal costs 11.7¢ per mile. However, home charging at the US average ($0.182/kWh = 5.2¢/mi) or overnight off-peak ($0.08/kWh = 2.5¢/mi) is 60% to 80% cheaper than gasoline.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-base font-black text-gray-950 mb-2">What are Tesla Supercharger idle fees and congestion fees?</h3>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Tesla charges an idle fee of $0.50 per minute once charging finishes if the station is at least 50% occupied, increasing to $1.00 per minute if the station is 100% full. The fee is waived if you disconnect within 5 minutes. Additionally, at select congested Superchargers, Tesla imposes a $1.00 per minute congestion fee if you continue charging past an 80% battery state of charge (SoC).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="p-5 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <h3 class="text-base font-black text-gray-950 mb-2">Can Ford, Chevy, Rivian, and Hyundai EVs use Tesla Superchargers in 2026?</h3>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-700 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Yes. Most major automakers—including Ford, General Motors (Chevy, GMC, Cadillac), Rivian, Hyundai, Kia, and Genesis—have official software access to over 17,800 Tesla V3 and V4 Supercharger stalls across North America. Drivers can charge using an OEM-approved NACS-to-CCS1 adapter and activate stalls directly via the Tesla smartphone app or their vehicle's native companion app.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

